WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.087 --> 00:00:02.570
(tranquil music)

2
00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.433
The problem is not democracy.

3
00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.470
At this point, our system is so corrupt.

4
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.741
It's become a system of legalized bribery.

5
00:00:09.741 --> 00:00:12.750 line:15%
(logo whooshes)

6
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:13.583 line:15%
People know it.

7
00:00:13.583 --> 00:00:16.220 line:15%
It's not like people are not aware,

8
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.460
and it's not like people are not upset,

9
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.990
but we're all living with this conundrum:

10
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:20.940
what do we do?

11
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.770
We are now at a point where it's not just

12
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.510
that we must move in another direction,

13
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.950
but we must move quickly.

14
00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.670
We have to become as convicted behind our love

15
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.484
as some people are convicted behind their hate.

16
00:00:33.484 --> 00:00:34.810
(gust whooshes)

17
00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.950
Greetings, internet, it is I, Rich Roll, your host.

18
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.840
Welcome to the podcast.

19
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.920
My guest today is a teacher.

20
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.870
She's an activist, a thought leader,

21
00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.560
and the author of 14 books, 4 of which

22
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.530
have been number-one "New York Times" bestsellers.

23
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.330
Her name is Marianne Williamson,

24
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.360
and if that name sounds familiar,

25
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.960
it might be because she's an absolute legend

26
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.650
in spiritual circles, but more likely,

27
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:04.120
it's because she quite famously ran for presidents in 2020,

28
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.750
so this is a conversation

29
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.620
about that fascinating chapter in her life,

30
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.290
but more broadly,

31
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.600
it's also about what's required

32
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.360
to solve our most urgent problems.

33
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.200
It's about the perils of our entrenched

34
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.420
corporate- slash government- slash media-industrial complex.

35
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.670
It's about the legacy of '60s activism,

36
00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.690
the role of spirituality in politics, the relationship

37
00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.880
between personal evolution and global change

38
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.880
and many other topics.

39
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.170
Marianne is a badass.

40
00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.670
I really enjoyed this one,

41
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.620 line:15%
so please, click that subscribe button,

42
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.720 line:15%
hit that notification bell,

43
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.980 line:15%
and enjoy what I think

44
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.350 line:15%
is a very insightful and fascinating conversation

45
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.920
with the force of nature

46
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.010
known as Marianne Williamson.

47
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.700
Marianne, I'm so happy to have you here today.

48
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.533
Welcome.
Thank you.

49
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:55.366
Thank you for coming.
Thank you,

50
00:01:55.366 --> 00:01:56.325
I'm happy to be here, honored.

51
00:01:56.325 --> 00:01:58.190
Yeah, I been looking forward to this for a long time.

52
00:01:58.190 --> 00:01:59.370
Thank you.

53
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.350
So many threads that we can pull on,

54
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.900
but I think a good place to start

55
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.480
is just talking

56
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.930
about where we find ourselves right now.

57
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.613 line:15%
Obviously, we live in very interesting times.

58
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.330 line:15%
It's a very unique period

59
00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.590 line:15%
that I haven't experienced in my lifetime,

60
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.110
in terms of the division and the divisiveness

61
00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.600
and the acrimony and this era of social media,

62
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.730
so I suppose I'm just curious:

63
00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.272
how are you feeling

64
00:02:26.272 --> 00:02:28.050
about this current moment
(Marianne chuckles)

65
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.190
that we find ourselves in?

66
00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.870
I think all of us are feeling rather discombobulated

67
00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.080
by the whole thing,

68
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.980
but what we're thinking about, I think, is very important.

69
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.320
I think we're living in two simultaneous realities.

70
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.340
I think, on one hand, it is the fall of Rome.

71
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.380
On one hand, it is the dissembling of a civilization,

72
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.230
a cratering of American civilization, in many ways.

73
00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.610
And on the other hand, it is the dawning

74
00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.220
and the struggling to be born of a new world,

75
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.053
a new renaissance.

76
00:02:56.053 --> 00:02:58.070
I think they're both true,

77
00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.890
and I think that we are called upon

78
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:03.350
to be both death doulas and birth doulas.

79
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.750
It's our job to help that which, in many ways, has to die

80
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:11.720
to die tenderly and with as little harm as possible

81
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.410
and with as just a transition as possible

82
00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.810
and to passionately and vigorously give birth

83
00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.363
to a world that works.

84
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.920
It's just like when we were children,

85
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.240
and we were taught about evolution.

86
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.890
If a species moves in a direction

87
00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:31.790
where its collective behavior is increasingly maladaptive

88
00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.220
for its survival,

89
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:34.580
one of two things will happen:

90
00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.500
either that species will go extinct,

91
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.280
or a mutation will occur.

92
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.070
It will evolve in a different direction,

93
00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.370
and I think that that's what's happened in many ways

94
00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.840
to our democracy, and certainly, it's happened

95
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.220
to the entirety of the human race.

96
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.550
We're moving in directions that are increasingly maladaptive

97
00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.370
for the survival of the species.

98
00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.540
We will now turn.

99
00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.590
We will now move in a different direction.

100
00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.930
We will now mutate and evolve,

101
00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.080
or global cataclysm,

102
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.750
catastrophe on a level that most of us can't even imagine,

103
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.180
is a very real possibility.

104
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.800
Yeah, it does feel like this epic arms race

105
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.180
of light versus dark,

106
00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.750
which is obviously a touchdown theme

107
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.233
in your teachings and what you talk about.

108
00:04:19.282 --> 00:04:23.180
If we root this in the political landscape,

109
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.340
the difference in the tenor of our moment

110
00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.113
is this ticking clock in the background, right?

111
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.120
We could quibble

112
00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.730
about policy changes and the direction of the country

113
00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.900
and where we're at as a collective consciousness

114
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.710
and address those items

115
00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.230
in a kind of incremental way,

116
00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.270
but now we have this looming existential threat

117
00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.163
that elevates the urgency of all of this

118
00:04:47.163 --> 00:04:50.150
and, I think, also exacerbates the antipathy

119
00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.400
and everything else that's going on right now

120
00:04:53.722 --> 00:04:56.290
that makes it this crucible

121
00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:57.820
where it's very difficult

122
00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.300
to figure out the best way forward.

123
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.890
And I'd like that idea that you mentioned

124
00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.720
around evolution of the individual

125
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.600
and how that is reflected

126
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.330
in society at large.

127
00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:18.330
It's this idea of ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny.

128
00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.670
You can't evolve the whole

129
00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.900
without the evolution of the individual.

130
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.880
I think the issue of incrementalism that you mentioned

131
00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:30.620
is very relevant.

132
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.100
I think it was in the "Letter from the Birmingham Jail"

133
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.210
that Martin Luther King talked about incrementalism,

134
00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.730
and he said, "If you take an incremental approach to change,

135
00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.150
then the status quo,

136
00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.560
which will never seek to disrupt itself,

137
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:46.180
will simply co-opt the ideas."

138
00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.030
What's happening right now

139
00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:52.270
is that even those who are standing for workable solutions

140
00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.000
and a more sustainable future...

141
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.940
There's a tendency of the economic and the political system

142
00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.290
to provide too little, too slow, too late.

143
00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.080
We have taken it to a point now...

144
00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.850
So many of the solutions being offered,

145
00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.120
for instance, even right now

146
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.900
by the Biden presidency, et cetera,

147
00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.210
they shoulda been offered in 1995.

148
00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:11.930
We are now at a point

149
00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.780
where it's not just that we must move in another direction,

150
00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.590
but we must move quick

151
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.183
because as you said, the risk is so great.

152
00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.920
When I think of things like protest against the Vietnam War,

153
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.770
passionate, important, huge,

154
00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:32.410
but when we thought about the worst that could happen,

155
00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.840
global cataclysm was not the worst that could happen.

156
00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.940
We're now living at a time

157
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.140
where the worst that could happen, global catastrophe

158
00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.460
on a level, like I said, we can't even imagine.

159
00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.780
It's a complete shift psychologically, I think,

160
00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.770
that we have to move into,

161
00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:51.140
and if you make that move without any consideration

162
00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:55.670
of larger, more expanded sense of possibility,

163
00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.210
almost from a metaphysical perspective,

164
00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.280
then you were left

165
00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.790
with understandable nihilism, cynicism, anger, and fear

166
00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.660
because if you only look at this moment

167
00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.590
from a rational perspective,

168
00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:09.880
it could be argued it's over.

169
00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.363
It could be argued that the gig is up.

170
00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.810
I think of it much like when the Israelites were standing

171
00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:17.240
at the Red Sea.

172
00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.460
If they go forward, they'll drown.

173
00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:20.650
If they go back...

174
00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.750
Pharaoh had decided he wanted his slaves back

175
00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.210
and sent his soldiers to go get them

176
00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.720
or to kill them if they refuse to come.

177
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.550
That story, like all stories of miraculous transformation,

178
00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.730
indicate that there's a level of consciousness

179
00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.540
at which the laws of time and space,

180
00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:37.443
as we know them, are transcended.

181
00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:44.190
If you think of the abolitionist movement,

182
00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.650
there was no reason

183
00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.790
from a rational perspective

184
00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.830
to think that slavery could be abolished,

185
00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:55.640
given how ensconced it was within the economy of the South.

186
00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:57.890
If you look at the women's suffragette movement,

187
00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:02.890
there was no reason to think that women could gain suffrage,

188
00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.960
given how infused the system was

189
00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.500
with the institutionalized oppression and suppression

190
00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.010
of women's rights.

191
00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:12.950
Even if you look at the civil rights movement,

192
00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:15.670
there was no reason from a rational perspective

193
00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.140
to think that it could succeed,

194
00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:22.400
given how embedded the systemic racism

195
00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.580
and institutionalized suppression

196
00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.840
of the rights of black people were

197
00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.890
within the systems of segregation in the American South,

198
00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.840
and yet these changes occur.

199
00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.260
They have occurred in American history,

200
00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.070
and they've occurred throughout the world.

201
00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:38.690
And I think we need to keep our eyes on those moments

202
00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:43.030
where very (snaps) quickly

203
00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:45.950
in a way that no (snaps) one might've predicted,

204
00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.840
the right prevailed, love prevailed,

205
00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.900
justice prevailed, mercy prevailed

206
00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:55.740
that there was such a burst of yearning and passion

207
00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.400
for those things within enough human hearts

208
00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.750
that humanity moved in that direction.

209
00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.740
Yeah, I suppose there is a seed of hopefulness

210
00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.060
that can be mined

211
00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.400
in thinking about those instances,

212
00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.560
but I can't help but reflect

213
00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.910
upon the inadequacies

214
00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:14.400
of a democratic system

215
00:09:15.238 --> 00:09:17.160
that is systemically ensconced

216
00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:18.870
in an incremental way

217
00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.810
of progressing change

218
00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.230
with this ticking clock and this existential...

219
00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.410
Do we have enough time?

220
00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:28.890
Is there adequate political will?

221
00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:31.640
Is there too much denial?

222
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.830
Is the corporate stranglehold

223
00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:37.810
on that political will

224
00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:41.410
too powerful to really create the change

225
00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:43.810
that we're gonna need in order to survive (laughs)

226
00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:46.740
as a species and as a thriving planet.

227
00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.140
Well, let's deconstruct that.

228
00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.110
The problem is not democracy.

229
00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:54.080
The problem is the anti-democratic force field

230
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.000
represented by the corporatized political duopoly

231
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.570
that now is keeping the will of the people

232
00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.530
from being expressed in political terms.

233
00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.530
If you look at issue after issue,

234
00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:07.500
the problem is not the American people.

235
00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:08.980
If you look at issue after issue,

236
00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.620
the consciousness of the American people is not the problem.

237
00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.000
The problem is how often the will of the American people,

238
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.240
because of gerrymandering,

239
00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:17.880
because of the corporatized forces

240
00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.260
within both political parties

241
00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.380
that peripheralize any non-corporate-backed candidates,

242
00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:25.613
now voter suppression, rights,

243
00:10:26.455 --> 00:10:29.250
and, first and foremost, the money,

244
00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.730
the undue influence of money on our politics,

245
00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:33.590
so democracy is the answer.

246
00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.940
The problem is that our democracy is not now functioning

247
00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.210
as a democracy.
Right, old-school democracy,

248
00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:39.479
democracy as originally-

249
00:10:39.479 --> 00:10:43.560
Well, we've never been perfect, but yeah,

250
00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:45.640
at this point, our system is so corrupt

251
00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.073
it's become a system of legalized bribery.

252
00:10:49.346 --> 00:10:50.230
People know it.

253
00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.060
It's not like people are not aware,

254
00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.260
and it's not like people are not upset,

255
00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.800
but we're all living with this conundrum:

256
00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:57.633
what do we do?

257
00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.710
Yeah, the distinction between the voters

258
00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.810
and the systemic ills

259
00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:08.010
of a democratic system that's been co-opted

260
00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:09.430
by corporate interests,

261
00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:12.820
so in looking at that problem or that dilemma,

262
00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:15.030
how do we untangle that knot

263
00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:19.600
and get the democratic system back on the rails

264
00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.600
so that it can function in the manner

265
00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.140
in which it was originally conceived

266
00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:25.550
and not be held hostage

267
00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.410
by the corporations

268
00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:29.860
that seem to dictate policy

269
00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.110
on every level?

270
00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.360
Well, with the current make-up of the Supreme Court,

271
00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:35.970
we certainly can assume

272
00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.843
that Citizens United will not be overturned anytime soon.

273
00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.100
In "Letters to a Young Poet,"

274
00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.690
I'm sure, a book that you're well aware of by Rilke,

275
00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.420
he says, "There are times when you don't know the answer

276
00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:49.430
but you live with the question,"

277
00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:50.863
and that is a question,

278
00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:53.010
the one you just posed,

279
00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:56.470
that every thinking person that I know

280
00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:59.427
is dealing with right now: "What do we do in 2024?"

281
00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:03.340
The way I look at it,

282
00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.940
the Republican Party as it now stands

283
00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.450
represents a nose-dive for our democracy,

284
00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:10.730
and the Democratic Party as it now stands,

285
00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:13.770
certainly under the leadership of its corporatized forces,

286
00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:17.203
represents a managed decline of American democracy.

287
00:12:18.244 --> 00:12:19.210
We all know the risk.

288
00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:21.980
You go third party, and you could risk

289
00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:26.390
helping neo-fascist forces get back into power.

290
00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.080
You work within the Democratic Party,

291
00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.540
and you see what they did to Bernie twice.

292
00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:31.890
I know what they did to me.

293
00:12:33.328 --> 00:12:36.673
(tuts) What is the way forward?

294
00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.160
I'm living with the question in my heart

295
00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:41.160
and most people I know

296
00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.800
are living with the question in their hearts.

297
00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.280
I think that the answers are going to emerge

298
00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:49.310
because I think the anguish of that question

299
00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:53.280
exists in enough of us

300
00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.040
and very serious people are doing

301
00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:59.000
very serious thinking and reflection on this question.

302
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.440
Yeah, there's many ways to dissect this.

303
00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.900
We could just look at the two-party duopoly

304
00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:09.110
and have a exploration of the possibility of a third party

305
00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:10.750
like Andrew Yang is doing right now,

306
00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:12.840
and he was in here recently talking about that,

307
00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:17.540
and he's got a series of ideas about how to untie this knot.

308
00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:19.790
We can look at incremental policy decisions,

309
00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:21.420
but ultimately, I think

310
00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:24.740
the conversation that I wanna have

311
00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.950
and where I think you are uniquely suited to speak to

312
00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.820
is the broader conversation

313
00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:34.270
about the crisis of consciousness

314
00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.300
that we're experiencing right now,

315
00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:37.640
a situation in which

316
00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.530
materialism and consumerism drive priorities.

317
00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:41.530
It's all buttressed

318
00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:43.670
by this government-industrial complex

319
00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.970
that's propped up by corporate interests

320
00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:48.610
that places accumulation and comfort

321
00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:50.940
above community and conservation,

322
00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.523
all at the cost of our wellbeing, of course.

323
00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.270
It's all being denigrated by social media

324
00:13:57.270 --> 00:14:01.320
and these algorithms that foment division and hate,

325
00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.420
ultimately dividing us

326
00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:08.270
from each other and from ourselves

327
00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:12.160
and distancing ourselves from the shared fabric

328
00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:17.160
that unites us and creates the cohesion that's required

329
00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:19.790
to function as a healthy society.

330
00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:23.100
I think that there are two points of demarcation

331
00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.200
that are worth noting:

332
00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.530
one, the advent of the Industrial Revolution

333
00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.870
and, second, the advent of trickle-down economics.

334
00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:32.440
It was in the late 1800s

335
00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.660
that the Industrial Revolution exploded onto the scene

336
00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:38.060
in England, then in the United States,

337
00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.980
and there were quite a few artists

338
00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:44.010
and philosophers, writers, thinkers,

339
00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.790
both in Europe and in the United States

340
00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:49.830
who tried to sound an alarm, a warning,

341
00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.530
that we were becoming, as a civilization,

342
00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.120
so mesmerized by externalities

343
00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.720
that we were losing the balance

344
00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.190
between the outer and the inner self.

345
00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:02.870
We were losing the balance

346
00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:05.920
with internal issues that were just as important

347
00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:07.960
as what we could see with our physical eyes.

348
00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.410
When I was in college,

349
00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:13.540
I had these huge posters on my wall.

350
00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:14.720
Remember when we used to have,

351
00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:17.090
from an art museum,

352
00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:18.380
art posters, right?

353
00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.253
And I had these huge angels,

354
00:15:22.126 --> 00:15:23.850
and it said be Burne-Jones,

355
00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:25.680
but I didn't know who Burne-Jones was.

356
00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.350
I just knew I loved these huge pictures of angels.

357
00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:29.730
Many years later I was walking

358
00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:31.520
down Fifth Avenue, New York City,

359
00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:34.020
passing the Metropolitan Museum,

360
00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:35.787
and they have these big flags:

361
00:15:35.787 --> 00:15:37.550
"This is what our exhibit is today."

362
00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:39.720
And there were these huge angels

363
00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.690
by Edward Burne-Jones.

364
00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.400
I was so excited, and I was gonna see them.

365
00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:47.600
I took one of those...

366
00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.350
You have that little machine, a little earphone.

367
00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:51.080
They tell you things.

368
00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:53.337
And I already started writing my book

369
00:15:53.337 --> 00:15:55.120
"Healing the Soul of America."

370
00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.040
I knew about the transcendentalists,

371
00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.360
and I knew about the Industrial Revolution and all of this,

372
00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:03.717
yet I had had no idea that his paintings were part of this.

373
00:16:03.717 --> 00:16:05.777
This is what Burnes-Jones said:

374
00:16:05.777 --> 00:16:08.000
"Every time they build a machine,

375
00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.131
I will paint an angel." (chuckles)

376
00:16:10.131 --> 00:16:11.200
(Rich laughs)

377
00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:12.290
He should be painting a lotta angels.

378
00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:13.800
Yeah, he had to paint a lot.

379
00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:14.940
This was the beginning

380
00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:16.810
of this horrifying split

381
00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:18.170
within human consciousness,

382
00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:19.770
and then by the 20th century,

383
00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.520
it climaxed with the Industrial Revolution

384
00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:26.230
and this idea that we would solve all of humanities problems

385
00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:28.420
through this mechanistic paradigm,

386
00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:31.470
the Newtonian idea that the world is just a machine

387
00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.530
and if you wanna fix it, you just tweak the machine.

388
00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:38.410
Well, obviously that has turned out to not be true,

389
00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:40.070
but the damage has been done

390
00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:43.260
in terms of so much of particularly the Western mind

391
00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:46.810
being drawn so far into the material world

392
00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:51.630
that it has almost withered our spiritual musculature.

393
00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:55.490
Nothing is more of an example of this

394
00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:57.040
than what happened in 1980.

395
00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.900
Now, I'm not romanticizing American capitalism before 1980.

396
00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.930
I'm not romanticizing or whitewashing the behavior

397
00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:06.780
of corporate interest in this country before 1980,

398
00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:09.950
but I can tell you, I'm old enough to be able to tell you,

399
00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:13.210
that there was a time in my lifetime...

400
00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:15.450
Not that we were perfect,

401
00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.460
not that our democracy was perfect,

402
00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:21.740
not that corporate ethics were so exemplary,

403
00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:24.640
but the social consensus was

404
00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.723
we were supposed to try.

405
00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.340
With the advent of trickle-down economics,

406
00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:34.433
this propaganda, this horrifying canard,

407
00:17:35.358 --> 00:17:38.010
was like wool pulled over the eyes of the American people,

408
00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:42.960
where "if we just increase the stockholder value,

409
00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:46.240
if we just increase the profits,

410
00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:49.093
the short-term profits of your stockholders,

411
00:17:49.929 --> 00:17:52.080
even if at the expense of the other stakeholders,

412
00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.410
at the expense of workers,

413
00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:56.070
at the expense of the community,

414
00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:58.000
at the expense of the environment,

415
00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.890
this'll be good, see,

416
00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:02.093
because it'll lift all boats.

417
00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.090
Those new corporate aristocrats will just create

418
00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:07.470
so many jobs

419
00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:09.730
that money will just trickle down, lift all boats."

420
00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:11.120
Well, obviously, after 40 years,

421
00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:13.770
we can see it did not lift all boats.

422
00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.720
It left millions and millions of people

423
00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.823
without even a life vest.

424
00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:22.430
I think a lotta people don't realize, in the 1970s,

425
00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.190
the average American worker had a decent job

426
00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:26.620
with decent benefits,

427
00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:29.180
could afford a car, could afford a home,

428
00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:30.770
could afford a yearly vacation,

429
00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:33.680
and could afford to send their kids to college.

430
00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:37.740
This is aberrational, this massive transfer of wealth.

431
00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.150
It began with Ronald Reagan.

432
00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:41.420
It began with the Republicans,

433
00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:44.660
but no Democratic president has stopped it,

434
00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:48.580
and we're now at a point where, as I said earlier,

435
00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.770
the status quo will not disrupt itself.

436
00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:52.700
It's no different than...

437
00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:56.500
It's interesting, Rich, because you and I are coming

438
00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:59.220
from the wellness community, et cetera.

439
00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:02.920
There is very sophisticated thinking within that community

440
00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:04.810
regarding drugs and alcohol,

441
00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.050
and it's very sophisticated

442
00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:09.290
because people have known so many people who died.

443
00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:12.230
And we know that if it goes too far...

444
00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:16.330
Most of us have had this situation in our lives.

445
00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:17.580
Either we've made the call

446
00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:19.427
or had someone else make the call to us:

447
00:19:19.427 --> 00:19:21.470
"You think we oughta do something,

448
00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.520
because if we don't intervene this person could die."

449
00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:27.830
We need to stop our magical thinking about democracy

450
00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:29.700
and about the survival of the human race.

451
00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:31.770
You continue like this, you could die;

452
00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.770
We will lose our democracy.

453
00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:36.610
So at this point, we need to stage an intervention,

454
00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:38.157
and as John F. Kennedy said,

455
00:19:38.157 --> 00:19:41.580
"Those who make peaceful revolution impossible

456
00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.970
make violent revolution inevitable,"

457
00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:45.630
and that to me is the point where we are.

458
00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:49.270
(tuts) Yeah, we can identify,

459
00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.800
or we can place all our focus on Donald Trump

460
00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:55.560
and label him as a malignant narcissist and the like,

461
00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:56.660
but ultimately,

462
00:19:56.660 --> 00:19:57.493
he's a symbol.
He's a symptom.

463
00:19:57.493 --> 00:20:00.830
He's a reflection of decades

464
00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.550
of tectonic economic shifts

465
00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:05.460
that have led to a situation

466
00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:08.120
where people who have been so deprived and unheard

467
00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:09.430
for so long

468
00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:10.930
and have to work three jobs,

469
00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:12.290
as opposed to the one job

470
00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:14.650
that provides the car and the picket fence

471
00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:15.920
and all of that...

472
00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:20.840
Of course, you're gonna have anger and resentment

473
00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:24.493
and all of the emotions

474
00:20:24.493 --> 00:20:26.130
that we're seeing flaring up.

475
00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:29.490
It's reflective of that.

476
00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:30.390
It's either gonna lead

477
00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:32.920
towards some kind of revolutionary act

478
00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.750
and the dissolution (laughs) of the union.

479
00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:38.510
Ultimately, there has to be a reckoning, right?

480
00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.760
The most healthy way to address it

481
00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:42.870
is to have some form of intervention

482
00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:45.130
where we can course-correct what has gone wrong

483
00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:48.080
and find a way to provide for those people

484
00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:49.130
in a meaningful way

485
00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.800
who have been so deprived for too long.

486
00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:53.990
If you look at history,

487
00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:55.040
it's very interesting

488
00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:04.370
to see not only what conditions provided the opportunity

489
00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:06.090
for Hitler to gain power

490
00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:08.430
but also how the United States and the other Allies

491
00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:09.910
responded to that problem

492
00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:11.660
at the end of the war.

493
00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.410
At the end of World War I,

494
00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.010
the attitude towards Germany on the part of the Allies

495
00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:19.060
was basically reparations in the form of Deutschmark

496
00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.900
till we tell you to stop.

497
00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.840
Interestingly enough, it was the American president

498
00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:24.780
at the time, Woodrow Wilson,

499
00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:27.390
who tried to warn them that this was a bad idea.

500
00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:32.280
He was not listened to, and the desperation

501
00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.740
of the German people at that time

502
00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:36.380
became a Petri dish.

503
00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.670
When you have large groups of desperate people,

504
00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:42.730
it becomes a national security risk, whether it's

505
00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:45.110
in a corner of the U.S. city or a corner of the world,

506
00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:48.280
because large groups of desperate people become a Petri dish

507
00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:51.460
out of which a certain level of societal dysfunction

508
00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:53.240
is almost inevitable.

509
00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:56.310
Ideological capture by genuinely psychotic forces

510
00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:58.710
becomes pretty easy.

511
00:21:58.710 --> 00:21:59.870
That's why the Marshall Plan.

512
00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:02.760
That's why we treated Germany so well after World War II.

513
00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:05.530
That's why we treated the Japanese

514
00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:06.880
so well after World War II.

515
00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:09.480
We knew that when people have been defeated,

516
00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.170
don't keep kicking them,

517
00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:13.550
something terrible is going to happen.

518
00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:15.543
Even if you don't see it from a moral perspective,

519
00:22:15.543 --> 00:22:17.890
just see it from a political perspective.

520
00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:19.210
So you're absolute, right?

521
00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:23.447
For 40 years, people have been kicked down.

522
00:22:23.447 --> 00:22:24.957
"Vote for us, and it'll be better,"

523
00:22:24.957 --> 00:22:26.680
"vote for us, and it'll be better,"

524
00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:28.123
and at a certain point,

525
00:22:30.078 --> 00:22:32.230
one man, who I don't think any of us thought

526
00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:35.270
could've done that much damage so quickly,

527
00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:37.140
who was willing to take advantage

528
00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.640
of all the anger, all the anger,

529
00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:42.520
to harness that for his own political purposes,

530
00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:45.000
that coupled with what you mentioned,

531
00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.840
the power of social media,

532
00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:51.050
and we've gotten not only the problem we have on our hands

533
00:22:51.050 --> 00:22:53.770
of a genuinely neo-fascist force field

534
00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:56.633
but what should have been seen as a predictable one.

535
00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:01.170
I believe if the Democratic Party had been truly holding

536
00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:04.350
to its principles over the last 40 years...

537
00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:07.390
Well, listen, if either political party had held

538
